# Advanced configuration -- common overrides and illustrative examples.
# Some entries below show the in-code default (uncomment only to
# override); others are placeholder examples for opt-in settings that
# have no meaningful default (multi-repo `REPOS`, the verify gate,
# event / analytics sinks). The orchestrator runs fine when none of
# these are set. The full reference -- every knob, every default --
# lives in `docs/configuration.md` and `docs/configuration/`.

# --- Target repository ---

# REPO and TARGET_REPO_ROOT live in `.env.example` -- they are the
# basic knobs needed to point the orchestrator at any repo.

# Base branch in the target repo (PR base).
# BASE_BRANCH=main

# Name of the git remote in TARGET_REPO_ROOT that points at REPO.
# REMOTE_NAME=origin

# Multi-repo mode. One entry per `;` separator, each
# `owner/name|target_root|base_branch[|remote_name[|parallel_limit]]`.
# Overrides REPO / TARGET_REPO_ROOT / BASE_BRANCH / REMOTE_NAME when set.
# REPOS=owner1/repo1|/path/to/repo1|main;owner2/repo2|/path/to/repo2|master|private|2

# Where per-issue git worktrees are created.
# WORKTREES_DIR=../wt-orchestrator

# Base branch of the orchestrator's own repo (self-update path only).
# ORCHESTRATOR_BASE_BRANCH=main

# --- Agent CLIs ---

# Path to the codex / claude binaries. Override only if not on $PATH.
# CODEX_BIN=codex
# CLAUDE_BIN=claude

# git identity injected into every agent commit.
# AGENT_GIT_NAME=chipping-orchestrator
# AGENT_GIT_EMAIL=chipping-orchestrator@users.noreply.github.com

# Example DEV_AGENT spec with backend-CLI args (quoting follows shell rules):
# DEV_AGENT=codex -m gpt-5.5 -c 'model_reasoning_effort="xhigh"'

# --- Cadence and budgets ---

# Polling interval for the main loop, seconds.
# POLL_INTERVAL=60

# How many ticks apart the closed-issue recovery sweep runs. That sweep
# issues one closed-issue query per non-terminal workflow label PER REPO
# every tick; across many repos at a short POLL_INTERVAL it dominates
# request volume and exhausts GitHub's 5000 req/hour primary rate limit
# (symptom: repeated "403: Forbidden" + long "Setting next backoff to …s"
# stalls in orchestrator.log). 1 (default) runs it every tick; raise it
# (e.g. 4-5) on multi-repo hosts. Cost: an externally-merged/closed issue
# may take up to N-1 extra ticks to finalize to `done`, and a closed
# `discussion` waits the same for the verdict on its plan PR -- that
# sweep is the only pass that revisits it. See
# docs/configuration.md#github-rate-limits.
# CLOSED_ISSUE_SWEEP_EVERY_N_TICKS=1

# How many ticks apart an OPEN `workflow:blocked` / `workflow:umbrella` issue
# is dispatched. Both handlers only walk the dependency graph -- one label read
# per child, per waiting parent -- and spawn nothing, so polling them every
# tick spends GitHub requests on parents whose children have not moved. 5
# (default) runs them on the first tick and every fifth after it, counted the
# same way as CLOSED_ISSUE_SWEEP_EVERY_N_TICKS; on the ticks between they are
# not submitted at all. Cost: up to N ticks of latency before a child whose
# dependencies finished is activated, before a parent whose children all
# landed completes, and before drift on either is detected. `decomposing`,
# unlabeled pickup, and closed-owner cleanup keep their own cadence. 1 restores
# every-tick dispatch. Must be >= 1.
# DEPENDENCY_POLL_EVERY_N_TICKS=5

# How long apart the terminal-artifact maintenance passes run, seconds: the
# bounded reclamation of the worktrees and branches of finished issues. The
# polling loop fits one in between passes (never inside a tick) once this long
# has elapsed, and `python -m orchestrator --cleanup-terminal-artifacts` runs
# one on demand. A pass only runs on a host it can prove quiet -- no other
# orchestrator process live on it, scheduler admission closed, every worker and
# claim drained -- and otherwise defers without touching anything. Must be >= 1.
# Nothing is persisted, so a restart costs at most one extra pass, and an
# interrupted one needs no recovery. It writes no workflow state (no label, no
# pinned state, no comment); each candidate it decides about earns one line on
# the orchestrator.worktree_lifecycle log and one bounded
# `terminal_artifact_cleanup` record on the analytics sink below. Runbook:
# docs/configuration/operations.md#reclaiming-a-finished-issues-artifacts.
# TERMINAL_ARTIFACT_CLEANUP_INTERVAL_SECONDS=86400

# Optional local window the polling process's own terminal-artifact maintenance
# passes are scheduled in, and the IANA timezone its clock is read in. Strict
# 24-hour HH:MM-HH:MM with no spaces inside: start inclusive, end exclusive, an
# end earlier than the start crosses midnight (23:00-01:00), and identical
# endpoints are rejected. A set window takes precedence over
# TERMINAL_ARTIFACT_CLEANUP_INTERVAL_SECONDS for automatic scheduling and
# requires TERMINAL_ARTIFACT_CLEANUP_TIMEZONE to name a zone the host resolves;
# a malformed window or a blank or unknown timezone aborts at startup naming the
# setting. Unset or blank (the default) disables the window, leaves the timezone
# unread, and keeps the interval above with its own default and validation.
# `--cleanup-terminal-artifacts` runs when asked either way. Startup validation
# is so far the only reader of these two: the polling loop still schedules every
# automatic pass on the interval above.
# TERMINAL_ARTIFACT_CLEANUP_WINDOW=03:00-05:00
# TERMINAL_ARTIFACT_CLEANUP_TIMEZONE=Asia/Novosibirsk

# Wall-clock cap per agent invocation, seconds.
# AGENT_TIMEOUT=1800

# Wall-clock cap per reviewer invocation. Defaults to AGENT_TIMEOUT.
# REVIEW_TIMEOUT=1800

# Review/fix iterations before parking on awaiting_human.
# MAX_REVIEW_ROUNDS=3

# Auto-conflict-resolution rounds before parking on awaiting_human.
# MAX_CONFLICT_ROUNDS=3

# Fresh implementer spawns per issue per 24h window (0 = unbounded). An
# exhausted budget parks the issue for a human; the window elapsing does not
# lift that park on its own.
# MAX_RETRIES_PER_DAY=3

# Resumes of one dev session before it is retired and respawned fresh from
# durable state, so a growing transcript cannot creep into a "Prompt is too
# long" context overflow (0 = resume forever).
# DEV_SESSION_MAX_RESUMES=10

# Lifetime ceiling on the agent runs one issue may spend -- every agent exit
# charged to it, in every role and at every stage, not just the implementer
# spawns MAX_RETRIES_PER_DAY meters. The backstop under the per-stage budgets:
# they bound how often one road is retried, this bounds the whole issue, and
# unlike the daily budget it never reopens. 0 = unlimited. Must be >= 0.
# The issue keeps a durable ledger of what it has spent against this, and the
# count runs even at 0: no stage turns a run away against it yet.
# MAX_AGENT_RUNS_PER_ISSUE=50

# --- Parallelism ---

# Per-repo cap on concurrent in-flight issues per tick. Must be >= 1.
# MAX_PARALLEL_ISSUES_PER_REPO=1

# Global cap across all configured repos. Must be >= 1.
# MAX_PARALLEL_ISSUES_GLOBAL=3

# --- Stage toggles ---

# Kill switch for NEW decompositions. Truthy: 1, true, on, yes.
# Off routes unlabeled issues -- and any already on `decomposing` with
# nothing in flight -- straight to `implementing`, and stops a fresh
# committed candidate ever entering the size gate below. What it does NOT
# do is decide anything already in flight: a recorded late generation goes
# on being adjudicated, revised, split, cleaned up, cancelled, and
# restarted either way, and a candidate this issue already froze goes on
# being measured. The `question` and `discussion` labels you apply by hand
# keep running on DECOMPOSE_AGENT either way too.
# DECOMPOSE=on

# Size ceiling one implementation candidate may publish under, counted in
# the lines its prospective pull request ADDS (frozen remote base commit
# vs. the exact committed candidate commit, every path, binary content
# contributing nothing, moved files counted where they land, no
# generated / vendored / lockfile exemptions). A candidate exactly at the
# value publishes as usual; only one STRICTLY past it is adjudicated.
# Must be >= 1.
# MAX_ADDED_LINES=4000

# After VERDICT: APPROVED, squash the dev's commits on the PR branch into
# one subject-only commit and force-push with lease. Truthy: 1, true, on,
# yes. Off leaves the per-step history intact; a squash an earlier tick
# already made is still finished.
# SQUASH_ON_APPROVAL=on

# End the subject of each commit the orchestrator publishes onto a PR with
# ` (#N)`, N being that PR, so rebase-merged history links back to it.
# Truthy: 1, true, on, yes. Turn it off on a target repo that lands PRs
# with GitHub's "Squash and merge" and its default message: GitHub appends
# its own (#N) there, so a single-commit PR would carry the number twice.
# No publication road reads it yet, so no subject is suffixed either way.
# PR_REF_IN_SUBJECT=on

# Quiet window after the most recent PR/issue comment before resuming
# the dev session in `fixing`, seconds.
# IN_REVIEW_DEBOUNCE_SECONDS=600

# --- Local verify gate ---

# Ordered shell commands run in the per-issue worktree on
# VERDICT: APPROVED, before relabeling to `in_review`. Separate entries
# with `;`. Empty (default) skips the verify gate. Do NOT embed secret
# literals -- failures publish the command verbatim on the issue.
# VERIFY_COMMANDS=python3 -m pytest -q;ruff check .

# Per-command wall-clock cap for VERIFY_COMMANDS, seconds.
# VERIFY_TIMEOUT=600

# --- Observability ---

# Optional JSONL audit-event sink. One event per line; no built-in
# rotation -- pair with logrotate if you leave the orchestrator running.
# EVENT_LOG_PATH=/var/log/orchestrator/events.jsonl

# Project-local analytics JSONL sink. Defaults to LOG_DIR/analytics.jsonl.
# Set to empty / off / disabled / none to disable writes. This is also where
# the daily terminal-artifact maintenance pass reports what it did, one
# `terminal_artifact_cleanup` record per candidate; disabling the sink loses
# those records and changes nothing about what the pass deletes.
# ANALYTICS_LOG_PATH=

# Retention window for ANALYTICS_LOG_PATH, days (0 or negative = keep
# indefinitely).
# ANALYTICS_RETENTION_DAYS=90

# libpq URL for the analytics Postgres service in `analytics-db/`.
# Consumed by `python -m orchestrator.observability.analytics.sync.cli`
# and by the analytics dashboard; NOT read by the polling loop. The
# dashboard resolves it at startup, so a change only lands on the next
# launch of:
#   uv run streamlit run orchestrator/apps/analytics_dashboard.py
# ANALYTICS_DB_URL=postgresql://orchestrator:orchestrator@127.0.0.1:5432/orchestrator_analytics

# Skill-trigger tracking. Set to 1 / true / on / yes to fold each agent's
# triggered skills (skills_triggered / skills_triggered_count /
# skills_available / skills_evidence and the incidental pair
# skills_incidental / skills_incidental_count) into the agent_exit
# analytics record. Default off so records stay shape-compatible with
# installs that never opted in.
# TRACK_SKILL_TRIGGERS=on

# Opt-in per-run agent reasoning-trajectory sink: an independent JSONL
# file of the ordered tool calls / results and final output each tracked
# run produced, plus a run-level token-usage / cost summary (and a claude
# per-turn breakdown). Default OFF (unset / empty / off / disabled / none); any
# other value is the explicit path. Separate from ANALYTICS_LOG_PATH --
# never written there, never replayed into Postgres, never shown in the
# analytics dashboard; local filesystem only and observation-only, so the
# file is safe to delete without affecting workflow state. Browse it with
# the dedicated viewer (reads this file directly, no Postgres):
#   uv run streamlit run orchestrator/apps/trajectory_dashboard.py
# PRIVACY: redaction
# masks only secret-shaped env values (and the GitHub token), NOT
# issue/repo content -- records can carry issue text, quoted source, and
# agent reasoning in cleartext, so scope the file's permissions like the
# repos it mirrors.
# TRAJECTORY_LOG_PATH=/var/log/orchestrator/trajectories.jsonl

# Retention window for TRAJECTORY_LOG_PATH, days (0 or negative = keep
# indefinitely). The polling loop does NOT prune this sink yet, so
# retention/rotation is operator-driven -- pair the file with logrotate.
# TRAJECTORY_RETENTION_DAYS=90

# Dashboard parallel read fan-out. Set to 1 / true / on / yes to
# dispatch the dashboard's 14 widget reads across a ThreadPoolExecutor
# instead of issuing them sequentially. Each worker gets its own
# thread-local psycopg connection. Default off so an operator can A/B
# against the sequential baseline. Parsed once per dashboard process,
# so a change only lands on the next launch of:
#   uv run streamlit run orchestrator/apps/analytics_dashboard.py
# DASHBOARD_PARALLEL_READS=on
